WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
How do you count the cells packed inside a whole brain?

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.520
Older textbooks said glial support cells outnumber neurons 10 to 1.

3
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.720
Solid tissue hides its cells, and one patch can mislead.

4
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:18.393
One method keeps tissue intact.

5
00:00:19.793 --> 00:00:23.073
Researchers fix a region and cut thin serial sections.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:29.653
Systematic sampling yields unbiased cell numbers from sections, called stereology.

7
00:00:31.293 --> 00:00:36.733
Sections also show apoptosis, a regulated death where nuclei condense into dense

8
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:38.713
clumps called pyknotic nuclei.

9
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:42.867
For a whole brain, slicing every millimetre is slow.

10
00:00:44.207 --> 00:00:47.707
Another method dissolves the tissue so nuclei float free.

11
00:00:49.047 --> 00:00:54.527
Because this mixture is uniform in every direction, or isotropic, small samples

12
00:00:54.527 --> 00:00:55.527
represent the whole.

13
00:00:56.707 --> 00:01:01.747
Staining separates neuronal from non-neuronal nuclei: the isotropic fractionator.

14
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:06.427
Counts mislead when you confuse density with total number.

15
00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:10.027
Density is cells per area or volume.

16
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.607
Cortex volume equals height × area.

17
00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:19.287
From mouse to human, height gives a factor of 2, and area about 1,000.

18
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.987
2 × 1,000 = 2,000.

19
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:25.787
Almost all the gain is sideways.

20
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:28.753
Time hides a second trap.

21
00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:34.953
In Hamburger's raw counts, chick leg motor neurons peak just over 20,000.

22
00:01:36.073 --> 00:01:39.873
By about day 10 of incubation, roughly 12,000 remain.

23
00:01:41.193 --> 00:01:46.293
A low final count is a half-empty bakery shelf: never made, or made then lost.

24
00:01:47.853 --> 00:01:51.573
Reliable counts sample whole tissue without bias across time.

25
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:59.013
Test that idea. Suppose a mutant ganglion at birth holds far fewer neurons than

26
00:01:59.013 --> 00:02:03.673
normal. You can count serial sections at earlier ages too.

27
00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:09.033
If neurons were made normally and then lost, how should its curve compare with

28
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.033
normal?

29
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:20.437
Early on, the mutant matches normal, then drops as dying, pyknotic nuclei appear.

30
00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:23.697
If too few were made, it starts low.

31
00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:28.977
So researchers count across ages, marking dividing and dying cells.

32
00:02:30.317 --> 00:02:35.157
Stop counting too early, and a loss that is merely delayed can look like a lasting

33
00:02:35.157 --> 00:02:36.157
excess.

34
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.820
In 2009, Azevedo and colleagues used the isotropic fractionator on whole human

35
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:49.700
brains. Instead of 10 glia per neuron, non-neuronal cells, mostly glia, roughly

36
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.120
equalled neurons overall.

37
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.000
Glia outnumbered neurons in cerebral cortex; neurons far outnumbered glia in the

38
00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.000
cerebellum.

39
00:02:59.583 --> 00:03:05.523
In 1980, Rockel and colleagues, and in 2008, Herculano-Houzel and colleagues,

40
00:03:05.523 --> 00:03:07.303
counted cortical neurons.

41
00:03:08.663 --> 00:03:13.443
Their counts suggest neurons under each square millimetre differ only a few-fold

42
00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:14.783
between mouse and human.

43
00:03:16.403 --> 00:03:17.903
Area differs about a thousandfold.

44
00:03:19.323 --> 00:03:23.063
Extra neurons sit mostly in extra area, not taller columns.

45
00:03:24.183 --> 00:03:26.863
Counting across ages tracks connections too.

46
00:03:28.163 --> 00:03:34.283
In 1997, Peter Huttenlocher and Arun Dabholkar counted synapses, the connections

47
00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:36.663
between neurons, in human cortex.

48
00:03:38.203 --> 00:03:42.903
Synaptic density peaked and declined later in prefrontal than in auditory cortex,

49
00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:47.103
with pruning, the removal of synapses, into adolescence.

50
00:03:48.563 --> 00:03:51.783
Measure whole tissue without bias, and follow it through time.
